{
  "subtype": "predictive_feature_engineering",
  "focus_variable": "overall_rating",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "customer_service_responsiveness",
      "role": "predictor",
      "is_predictor": true,
      "rationale": "A qualitative service-handling dimension (responsive/proactive vs. dismissive/unresolved) plausibly extracted from the review text. It lies outside the Overall Rating focus, is not a deterministic function of it, and is not knowable only after the outcome (it describes an in-service experience). Service responsiveness is a well-known driver of airline satisfaction, so it plausibly carries predictive signal. It also captures complaint-resolution / responsiveness nuance not fully encoded by the existing numeric 'Staff Service' rating, so it can add incremental leverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "value_perception",
      "role": "predictor",
      "is_predictor": true,
      "rationale": "Perceived price fairness / value for money is a distinct construct from the overall satisfaction score (not a restatement, component, or deterministic function of Overall Rating), and it is strongly correlated with satisfaction, so it is a legitimate predictor of the focus. The caveat is that it is essentially a re-expression of the ALREADY-PRESENT 'Value For Money' original column; relative to the focus it is a predictor, but it adds little NEW predictive information beyond what the model already has, so its incremental utility is low."
    }
  ],
  "n_predictors": 2,
  "predictive_utility": 0.4,
  "rationale": "Both added columns are genuine predictors of Overall Rating rather than restatements or leakage of the focus itself: each is a separate experiential/perceptual dimension that plausibly drives satisfaction and is knowable at review time. However, incremental predictive leverage beyond the original columns is only moderate. 'customer_service_responsiveness' contributes the most new signal, since it encodes qualitative service-handling detail not fully captured by the numeric 'Staff Service' field. 'value_perception' is a legitimate driver but largely duplicates the existing 'Value For Money' column, which is one of the strongest predictors already available, so it adds little that a model doesn't already have. Net: a valid but partially redundant predictor set, hence moderate-to-low set-level utility."
}

